   "text": "What it is The capital system is every way wealth can be stored, moved and grown: money, debts, ownership stakes, physical things, digital tokens, and the skills and relationships inside you. This map lays them out as one connected tree. How the map is organised The tree is organised by what things are. Each branch is a kind of asset or a body of ideas. A page lives in one place on the tree and links to everything else it touches. One question sorts money and claims: whose liability is it, and what is it a claim on? See claims and liabilities. One model sorts stores of wealth by risk: the Cartography of Wealth, which maps each store by how embodied in you it is and how visible it is to the state, and names the one thing most likely to kill it. How things trade right now (for example, crypto moving like high growth tech shares) is a separate question. It changes with conditions, so it lives under market regimes rather than shaping the tree. The branches foundation — the ideas everything rests on. money — what money is, and its hierarchy from gold to bank deposits. personal capital — skills, health, reputation and network. fixed income, equities, real assets, digital assets, derivatives — the asset classes. portfolio — combining them so that no single failure clears the board. markets, measure, practice, tools — the plumbing, the scorecard and the practicalities. Key rule: this is a reference, not advice. It explains how things work so you can judge for yourself.",
