   "adv": false,
   "html": "<h2>What it is</h2>\n<p><strong>Fiat</strong> money is currency that a government declares legal tender and that can't be exchanged for a fixed amount of anything else. Nearly every national currency today is fiat.</p>\n<h2>Why people hold it</h2>\n<p>Fiat isn't \"backed\" by a commodity, but it isn't backed by nothing either. Its demand comes from:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Taxes.</strong> The state requires taxes to be paid in its own currency.</li>\n<li><strong>Debts.</strong> Loans, mortgages and contracts are written in it, so borrowers must get it to repay.</li>\n<li><strong>Prices and wages.</strong> It is the unit everyone keeps score in.</li>\n<li><strong>The central bank and the banking system</strong> standing behind it. See <a class=\"wl\" href=\"#/map/p/central-bank-money\" data-page=\"central-bank-money\">Central Bank Money</a>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Two different \"cash\" holdings</h2>\n<p>Cash straddles the line on the <a class=\"wl\" href=\"#/map/p/cartography-of-wealth\" data-page=\"cartography-of-wealth\">Cartography of Wealth</a>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Physical notes</strong> are bearer and private (<strong>Tier 2</strong>), but exposed to theft and loss.</li>\n<li><strong>Money in the bank</strong> is a <a class=\"wl\" href=\"#/map/p/bank-deposits\" data-page=\"bank-deposits\">bank deposit</a>: recorded, legible and protected by the state (<strong>Tier 3</strong>), but exposed to bank failure, freezes and capital controls.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>What goes wrong</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Inflation</strong> steadily reduces what each unit buys. After inflation, cash earning little or no interest loses value year after year. See <a class=\"wl\" href=\"#/map/p/inflation\" data-page=\"inflation\">Inflation</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Currency crises.</strong> When trust in a government breaks, its currency can lose most of its value quickly.</li>\n<li><strong>Capital controls</strong> can limit how much you withdraw or move abroad.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Key rule:</strong> cash is the best tool for spending and short-term safety, and one of the worst for storing wealth over decades.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<h2>Check yourself</h2>\n<div class=\"qa\"><p class=\"q\">Why isn't the US dollar in the same position as a memecoin, when neither is backed by anything?</p><details><summary>Show answer</summary><p>Because the dollar has forced demand (taxes, debts and global trade are written in it) and a central bank behind it. A memecoin has none of these. See <a class=\"wl\" href=\"#/map/p/hierarchy-of-money\" data-page=\"hierarchy-of-money\">The Hierarchy of Money</a>.</p></details></div>",
