   "traits": [],
   "path": [
    "capital-system",
    "money",
    "eurodollars"
   ],
   "branch": "money",
   "adv": true,
   "html": "<h2>What it is</h2>\n<p><strong>Eurodollars</strong> are US dollar deposits and loans held at banks outside the United States. The name is historical: the market started in Europe, but today it is global.</p>\n<p>A eurodollar is a <strong>promise by a foreign bank</strong> to pay dollars. Such banks usually have no direct access to the US central bank's lending, so they must find dollars in the market when they need them.</p>\n<h2>Why it matters</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Huge dollar debts outside the US.</strong> Companies and governments around the world borrow in dollars. They must earn or buy dollars to repay, which is a large source of global demand for the currency.</li>\n<li><strong>Dollar shortages.</strong> In a crisis, everyone owing dollars scrambles for them at once, and the dollar tends to rise even when the crisis started in the US.</li>\n<li><strong>Swap lines.</strong> The US central bank can lend dollars to other central banks in emergencies, which then lend them on to their banks.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Where it sits</h2>\n<p>In the <a class=\"wl\" href=\"#/map/p/hierarchy-of-money\" data-page=\"hierarchy-of-money\">hierarchy of money</a>, eurodollars sit above <a class=\"wl\" href=\"#/map/p/bank-deposits\" data-page=\"bank-deposits\">Bank Deposits</a> at US banks: a private, offshore promise to pay dollars, usually without a direct central bank backstop.</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Key rule:</strong> much of the world's dollar system runs outside the United States, which is why a global dollar squeeze can hit markets everywhere at once.</p>\n</blockquote>",
   "deep": "",
   "links": [
    "bank-deposits",
    "hierarchy-of-money"
   ],
   "stub": false,
   "text": "What it is Eurodollars are US dollar deposits and loans held at banks outside the United States. The name is historical: the market started in Europe, but today it is global. A eurodollar is a promise by a foreign bank to pay dollars. Such banks usually have no direct access to the US central bank's lending, so they must find dollars in the market when they need them. Why it matters Huge dollar debts outside the US. Companies and governments around the world borrow in dollars. They must earn or buy dollars to repay, which is a large source of global demand for the currency. Dollar shortages. In a crisis, everyone owing dollars scrambles for them at once, and the dollar tends to rise even when the crisis started in the US. Swap lines. The US central bank can lend dollars to other central banks in emergencies, which then lend them on to their banks. Where it sits In the hierarchy of money, eurodollars sit above bank deposits at US banks: a private, offshore promise to pay dollars, usually without a direct central bank backstop. Key rule: much of the world's dollar system runs outside the United States, which is why a global dollar squeeze can hit markets everywhere at once.",
   "textDeep": "",
